# Git Workflow for dd-code

You must follow this Git workflow when developing new features or fixing bugs.

## Branch Naming Convention

All branches must follow this naming pattern:
- Features: `feature/<issue-id>-<short-description>`
- Bug fixes: `fix/<issue-id>-<short-description>`
- Hotfixes: `hotfix/<issue-id>-<short-description>`

Examples:
- `feature/12-add-user-authentication`
- `fix/45-resolve-storage-sync-issue`
- `hotfix/67-critical-data-loss`

## Development Workflow

### Step 1: Sync with Main Branch

Before starting any new work, always pull the latest changes from main:

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
```

### Step 2: Create Feature Branch

Create a new branch from the updated main branch:

```bash
git checkout -b feature/<issue-id>-<short-description>
```

For bug fixes:
```bash
git checkout -b fix/<issue-id>-<short-description>
```

### Step 3: Development

- Make atomic commits with clear, descriptive messages
- Commit frequently with meaningful changes
- Keep commits focused on a single logical change

### Step 4: Commit Message Format

All commit messages MUST include the issue ID and follow this format:

```
<type>(#<issue-id>): <short description>

<optional longer description>

<optional footer>
```

**Types:**
- `feat`: New feature
- `fix`: Bug fix
- `refactor`: Code refactoring (no functional change)
- `docs`: Documentation changes
- `test`: Adding or updating tests
- `chore`: Build process, dependencies, or tooling changes
- `style`: Code style changes (formatting, whitespace)

**Examples:**
```
feat(#12): add user authentication with OAuth2

Implements OAuth2 flow with Google and GitHub providers.
Includes token refresh logic and session management.
```

```
fix(#45): resolve storage sync race condition

Added mutex lock to prevent concurrent write operations
that were causing data corruption.
```

```
refactor(#23): extract storage layer into separate module
```

### Step 5: Keep Branch Updated

Regularly sync your branch with main to avoid merge conflicts:

```bash
git fetch origin main
git rebase origin/main
```

Or if you prefer merging:
```bash
git fetch origin main
git merge origin/main
```

### Step 6: Push Branch

Push your branch to remote:

```bash
git push -u origin <branch-name>
```

For subsequent pushes:
```bash
git push
```

After rebasing, you may need to force push (use with caution):
```bash
git push --force-with-lease
```

### Step 7: Create Pull Request

When your feature is complete, create a Pull Request using the GitHub CLI:

```bash
gh pr create --title "<type>(#<issue-id>): <description>" --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
## Summary
- Brief description of changes
- List key changes made

## Issue
Closes #<issue-id>

## Type of Change
- [ ] New feature
- [ ] Bug fix
- [ ] Refactoring
- [ ] Documentation
- [ ] Tests

## Testing
- [ ] Unit tests added/updated
- [ ] Manual testing completed
- [ ] All existing tests pass

## Checklist
- [ ] Code follows project style guidelines
- [ ] Self-review completed
- [ ] Documentation updated if needed
EOF
)"
```

## Quick Reference Commands

```bash
# Start new feature
git checkout main && git pull && git checkout -b feature/<issue-id>-<desc>

# Start bug fix
git checkout main && git pull && git checkout -b fix/<issue-id>-<desc>

# Commit with issue reference
git commit -m "feat(#<issue-id>): <description>"

# Sync with main
git fetch origin main && git rebase origin/main

# Push and create PR
git push -u origin HEAD && gh pr create
```

## Parallel Development with Worktrees

When you need to work on multiple features simultaneously without switching branches:

```bash
# Create a worktree for a new feature
git worktree add ../dd-code-feature-<id> -b feature/<id>-<desc>

# Create a worktree for an existing branch
git worktree add ../dd-code-<branch-name> <branch-name>

# List all worktrees
git worktree list

# Remove a worktree when done
git worktree remove ../dd-code-<name>
```

This allows you to:
- Keep the dev server running on main in `dd-code/`
- Develop a feature in `dd-code-feature-<id>/`
- Work on a bug fix in `dd-code-fix-<id>/`

## Important Rules

1. **Never commit directly to main** - Always use feature branches
2. **Always include issue ID** - Every commit and PR must reference an issue
3. **Keep PRs focused** - One feature/fix per PR
4. **Write meaningful commit messages** - Future you will thank present you
5. **Rebase before PR** - Ensure your branch is up to date with main
6. **Request reviews** - Don't merge without at least one approval
